      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The exact mechanism for different propensities to obesity when consuming a high-fat diet (HFD) is largely unknown. Thyroid hormone (TH) is an important modulator of energy homeostasis and body weight."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "The present study aimed to find the potential mechanisms of TH in the development of obesity-prone (OP) and obesity-resistant (OR) mice after short-term and long-term HFD feeding."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "C57Bl/6 male mice were randomly divided into two groups: a low-fat diet (LFD) group and an HFD group. In the 7th week, HFD-fed mice were classified as OP or OR according to upper and lower tertiles of body weight. Half of the mice were sacrificed at this time point and the remaining mice were kept on feeding and sacrificed in the 27th week. Indirect calorimetry was performed. At harvest, serum was used for ELISA assays and oxidative stress biomarkers determination. Tissues were dissected for deiodinases activity and relative mRNA expression determination, as well as antioxidant capacity evaluation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In the 7th week, OP mice showed a significant body weight gain, decreased energy expenditure (EE), normal circulating TH levels, and activated HPT axis, whereas OR mice had normal body weight and maintained T(3) levels only through enhancing hepatic D1 activity. In the 27th week, OR mice gained more body weight than LFD mice accompanied by an activation of HPT axis and decreased hepatic deiodination. Genes involved in TH production were down-regulated in OP mice and up-regulated in OR mice. Changes in deiodinases activity and thyroid function were related with redox status in specific tissues. Furthermore, OP mice had more serious hepatic steatosis than OR mice, with up-regulation of T(3) target genes (e.g. Srebp1c, Acc1, Fasn) involved in lipid synthesis and down-regulation of Pgc1α, Cyp7a1 and Cpt1α."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "HPT axis function and deiodinases activity might be involved in different propensities to obesity and the ability of OR mice to resist obesity was limited."
          }
        ]
      },
